# The MIME Type for .eml Attachments: Why `message/rfc822` Is Correct

When you need to forward an MTA report or repackage a bounce message as an attachment, the immediate question is often about the Content-Type header. In your scenario, you are using PHP's IMAP library to fetch the raw body of a message and intend to attach it to a new email. You've likely encountered `message/rfc822` suggested in documentation, but found that Thunderbird displays it as an empty window or refuses to render the nested headers and bodies correctly. Conversely, setting it to `text/plain` renders the content as raw header lines and text blocks, which defeats the purpose of viewing it as a distinct email entity.

The correct MIME type for any file that is intended to be opened as an email client documentâwhether it's a standard `.eml` file or a forwarded MTA reportâis unequivocally `message/rfc822`. This is not merely a convention; it is the strict definition of how email clients should interpret the data. When you set the Content-Type to `message/rfc822`, the receiving mail client (like Thunderbird, Apple Mail, or Outlook) reads the first line of the file as a valid message header block. It expects to find an envelope-to-envelope boundary and attempts to render the entire structure recursively.

The confusion regarding the "empty popup" in Thunderbird usually stems from how modern clients handle `message/rfc822` attachments versus inline display. When you attach a file with the type `message/rfc822`, the client treats it as a nested email object. If the attachment is opened directly, the client attempts to parse the inner headers. If those inner headers are malformed or if the client expects an inline view rather than an attachment view, the rendering engine may fail to display the internal content, appearing empty. However, this is a client-side rendering behavior, not a MIME type error. The MIME type itself remains correct for encapsulating a full email message within another.

Using `text/plain` or `text/html` on an `.eml` file strips away the critical boundary between the outer wrapper and the inner message. An `.eml` file is essentially a text representation of an RFC 5322 compliant email message. It contains headers like `From:`, `To:`, `Subject:`, and `Message-ID:` at the very top, followed by a body (which might contain MIME boundaries if it itself has attachments). If you declare this as `text/plain`, you are telling the client to ignore the header block structure and treat everything after the first line as mere text. This is why you see the raw headers displayed as paragraphs of text rather than clickable or foldable email metadata.

To implement this correctly in your PHP environment, ensure that when constructing the MIME multipart message for the new email, the attachment part is explicitly defined with `message/rfc822`. The code logic generally looks like this:

```php
$headers = "From: sender@example.com\r\n";
$body = "--boundary\r\nContent-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8\r\n\r\nThis is the body.\r\n--boundary--";

$attachmentBody = $body_from_imap; // The raw string fetched via imap_fetchbody
$attachmentHeaders = "Content-Type: message/rfc822; name=\"bounce.eml\"\r\nContent-Disposition: attachment; filename=\"bounce.eml\"\r\n\r\n";

$mimeBoundary = "boundary=" . str_replace("=", "", bin2hex(random_bytes(16)));
$mixedBody = "--$mimeBoundary\r\n" . $headers . "\r\n\r\nThis is the main body.\r\n--$mimeBoundary\r\n" . $attachmentHeaders . $attachmentBody . "\r\n--$mimeBoundary--";

mail($recipient, $subject, $mixedBody, $headers);
```

The critical line here is `Content-Type: message/rfc822; name="bounce.eml"`. This tells the recipient's mail client that the attached content is not just a text file, but a complete email message that can be opened in its entirety.

If you are building an application where users frequently need to view MTA reports or bounce logs as native emails, you might consider providing a direct download link for `.eml` files rather than embedding them as attachments with `Content-Disposition: attachment`. When a user clicks a link to a file hosted on your server with the correct headers, their email client will open it in a new window, preserving the original rendering behavior. If you must embed it, ensure you are not stripping the newline characters (`\r\n`) that separate the header block from the body within the fetched string, as this can break the parser inside `message/rfc822` attachments.

While there is no official documentation specifically tied to LaravelCompany regarding this specific MIME type nuance, best practices for handling MTA reports and bounce messages remain consistent across the industry: treat `.eml` content strictly as nested messages. Do not attempt to render them inline as HTML or plain text; always preserve their encapsulation structure to ensure compatibility with diverse email clients ranging from mobile apps to desktop suites.
